-### Parameters and Edge Cases
-- **Voxel Size**: Must be positive. If the grid is non-uniform, this method requires modification (see [Computational Space Traversal](10339-computational-space-traversal.md)) [S1].
-- **Zero Direction Components**: If $d_i = 0$, the ray never crosses a boundary in that dimension. $t_{max, i}$ is set to infinity, and $\Delta t_i$ is effectively ignored in the min-selection [S5].
-- **Ray Origin Outside Grid**: The initialization step must clamp the starting voxel or handle the "entry into grid" case explicitly. If the ray starts outside, the first few $t_{max}$ values may be negative, indicating the ray has already passed those boundaries. The loop should typically begin only after the ray enters the grid bounding box [G].
-- **Precision Errors**: Floating-point inaccuracies can cause the ray to miss intersections or visit voxels incorrectly. [Robust Grid Traversal](#robust-grid-traversal) addresses this by retaining intersections even if they fall slightly outside the current cell due to precision errors [S2].
+**Parameters:**
+- Grid Resolution $(N_x, N_y, N_z)$: No analytical formula exists for the optimal resolution [S1]. Empirically, a resolution yielding roughly one particle per voxel achieves optimum performance for uniformly distributed particles [S1]. For the Bench scene with $2 \times 10^5$ particles, $60 \times 60 \times 60$ is optimal [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Zero Direction:** If a ray component is zero, $t_{delta}$ becomes infinite, and the ray never crosses a grid plane in that dimension. The code must handle division by zero [G].
+- **Precision Errors:** Numerical robustness errors can cause intersections on grid bounding boxes or implied planes to be missed if intersections outside the current cell are rejected [S2]. This happens when computing intersections while visiting a cell, but the result is rejected due to precision errors placing it outside that cell [S2].
+- **Low Resolution:** If grid resolution is much lower than optimal (e.g., $< 60 \times 60 \times 60$ for the Bench scene), performance is poor because too many particles reside in each voxel [S1].
 
